  "title": "Personal Information Exposure",
  "content": "Personal information exposure occurs when an ordinary post, photo, profile, or document reveals details that can support targeting or reconnaissance. The information may be public by design and still create risk when several small details are combined. Reviewing public information for defensive purposes is often called open-source intelligence, or OSINT.\n\n<b>What a photo can reveal</b>\n\nThe visible image may show badges, uniforms, equipment, entrances, workspaces, schedules, or names. Background details can identify where people gather and how access is controlled. The file or platform may also expose metadata such as the capture time, device information, or geographic coordinates. Even when a platform removes embedded location data, captions, landmarks, tags, and posting times can reveal the same facts.\n\nAn attacker does not need one dramatic secret. A location from one post, a shift time from another, and a badge color from a third can become a practical plan for impersonation or physical access. Repeated posts may also establish routines and show when a person or location is most vulnerable.\n\n<b>Reduce exposure before sharing</b>\n\nAsk whether the post reveals a current location, schedule, access method, identifier, or another person's information. Crop or blur badges, screens, documents, keys, QR codes, and sensitive background details. Disable unnecessary location tagging and review what metadata the chosen service preserves. Delay public posts from sensitive locations until the activity has ended, and use the organization's approved communication route for operational photos.\n\nIf exposed information is discovered, preserve the post details and report the risk rather than blaming the person who shared it. Removal may reduce future exposure, but responders should first record what was visible and consider whether access procedures, credentials, or public guidance must change. The goal is to make safe sharing easier, not to stop people from communicating.",
  "links": [
    "photo's metadata",
    "casual photo"
  ]
